{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Horizon-dependent bias: forecasts are slightly high at short horizons (≈D+1 to D+10, typically +0.2 to +1.0) but become too low at longer horizons when a rally persists (≈D+20 to D+30, often −2 to −6). Apply a bias that transitions from mildly negative early to positive late (i.e., lift the back end).",
    "Underreaction to regime shifts: when the last observed prices start trending up, the model mean-reverts too quickly and fails to carry momentum into weeks 3–4. Add a trend/momentum term based on the last 5–10 days (positive slope → raise D+15 to D+30).",
    "Peak/late-rally miss: the model systematically misses late spikes/accelerations (true outcomes jump into 71–73 while forecasts stay ~66–69). If recent volatility is rising and price is making higher highs, widen the upper tail and increase late-horizon levels (convex adjustment).",
    "Over-smoothing around local peaks: near short-term highs the model often predicts a gentle fade that is too strong; actual prices hold up better. Reduce mean-reversion strength when the 10–20d trend is positive and drawdowns are shallow.",
    "Volatility underestimation at long horizons: forecast paths are too flat versus realized swings. Increase forecast variance (or scenario spread) beyond ~2 weeks, especially following breakout days."
  ]
}
